- Maruti changing gears to race against aggressive new rivals, ETAuto: Market leader Maruti Suzuki is realigning its product and positioning strategy to take on aggressive new competition. The company also plans to expand and upgrade its range to tap the change in customer tastes while sticking to its core competence in smaller vehicles, Maruti Suzuki MD & CEO Kenichi Ayukawa said. NEW DELHI: Market leader Maruti Suzuki is realigning its product and positioning strategy to take on aggressive new competition in its bread and butter market. “We have competition for all our models but we aren’t terribly concerned because Suzuki is originally a small car player. Maruti’s 45 per cent market share in the small car business means the company doesn’t have a presence in the remaining 55 per cent of the market. The company will stick to its core market but offer a wider variety of products within that range. “We will stick to the A, B and C segments (mini, premium compact and compact SUV/sedan) but we will improve product offerings there.
- Maruti Suzuki likely to breach pre-covid sales peak in FY24 | Mint: New Delhi: Maruti Suzuki India Ltd, the country’s largest carmaker, is poised for a record-breaking year in FY24 as it seeks to regain its lost market share with the launch of new sport utility vehicles (SUVs) as well as its premium offerings. While most carmakers saw record passenger vehicle (PV) sales last year, Maruti Suzuki is yet to surpass its pre-covid domestic sales volume record of 1.753 million units in 2018-19. While the industry expects 5-7% growth this fiscal year on the back of new launches, Maruti Suzuki should be growing at a faster pace to surpass its highest-ever sales. For Maruti, the shift to categories with a higher share in overall PV sales, like SUVs, is strategic, as it expects the share of small cars to decline gradually. Despite small cars continuing to see significant sales in India, Maruti Suzuki, which accounts for two-thirds of passenger vehicle sales, expects the pace of growth to be slower compared to other segments, he added.
